## 7. Requirements

- REQ-FIG0902-01 [MUST-STANDARD] View A shows five key rows, one per vertex, in the order `s`, `a`, `b`, `c`, `t`, each row holding the neighbours of its vertex (source: chapter 9.1.1, adjacency list definition; vertex order from BRIEFING section 3).
- REQ-FIG0902-02 [MUST-STANDARD] Every neighbour entry in view A is an edge of the sample graph, and each row holds exactly its vertex's degree, so the five rows hold 2, 4, 3, 3 and 2 entries (source: BRIEFING section 2, verified degrees).
- REQ-FIG0902-03 [MUST-STANDARD] View B shows the matrix as five row headers by five column headers around one grid of 25 cells, headers in the same order as view A (source: chapter 9.1.1, V by V table).
- REQ-FIG0902-04 [MUST-STANDARD] Fourteen cells are filled, one per directed incidence of the seven piece edges, and every filled cell has a filled mirror across the diagonal, so the grid is symmetric (source: verified edge list of BRIEFING section 2, arithmetic in section 6).
- REQ-FIG0902-05 [MUST-STANDARD] A 1.6 diagonal rule with dash 6 4 separates the two halves and each half carries a 15 unit label, so the mirror is carried by position and shape and not by hue (source: BRIEFING section 5, hue always doubled by shape or label).
- REQ-FIG0902-06 [MUST-STANDARD] View C pairs space against query and quotes 16 entries against 64 cells, both counted on the whole sample graph, which the strip names once beside the pair (source: BRIEFING section 2, V + E 16 and V squared 64 on 8 vertices and 8 edges).
- REQ-FIG0902-07 [MUST-STANDARD] The same strip carries the asymptotic pair O(V+E) against O(V squared) credited to 9.1.1, and no single pair mixes a count from the piece with a count from the whole graph (source: chapter 9.1.1).
- REQ-FIG0902-08 [MUST-STANDARD] The query side states that the matrix answers a join test in one cell at O(1) and that the list answers it by scanning one row (source: chapter 9.1.1 for O(1); the scan follows from the list definition and is named as the operation).
- REQ-FIG0902-09 [MUST-LOCAL] A cell carries one of two states, filled with a 3.2 inset outline or empty with a 1.0 rule, and no cell fill varies with degree or count (source: PLAN.md forbidden list, no heat map).
- REQ-FIG0902-10 [MUST-LOCAL] The matrix cell is 62 units and the list row is 52 units, so the grid is the largest repeated structure on the sheet (source: PLAN.md dominant element, the matrix grid).
- REQ-FIG0902-11 [MUST-STANDARD] No type is below 14 canvas units and every stroke and fill is a role from section 5 (source: BRIEFING sections 4 and 5).

## 8. Composition plan

- dominant: the matrix grid, 310 by 310 at x 630 to 940 and y 126 to 436, whose cell is the largest repeated shape on the sheet.
- focal mechanism: the strongest junction is the edge between a filled cell and its empty neighbour inside the grid, where accent meets card across a hairline, read along the diagonal.
- hierarchy: grid fill area first, then the two space values in accent-ink, then the list rows, then the half labels and notes in muted.
- reading path: the panel labels at the top, down the five list rows, across to the grid, along the diagonal to the mirrored half, out at the strip's pair.
- balance: the sheet is asymmetric by design, the grid panel 468 wide against the list panel 420, and the full width strip below acts as the tie.
- framing: two framed panels on the top band, one unframed strip on the bottom band, with 28 units of empty sheet between the bands.
- negative space: 11 of the 25 grid cells stay empty and read as the argument; the margin outside the three panels stays empty so the grid keeps its weight.
- grouping: proximity binds each header to its grid, the zebra binds alternate rows, the leader binds "sample graph, 8 vertices" to the pair it counts.

## 9. Build contract

Steps, in order:

1. Set the viewBox and the sheet; place panel A at 32 to 452, panel B at 500 to 968, both from y 56 to 452, and the strip band at 32 to 968 from y 480 to 620.
2. Build view A: five rows of 52 units from y 118, key column at x 56, neighbour column at x 140, degree value right aligned at x 430.
3. Build view B: column headers at y 112, grid 310 by 310 at x 630 to 940 and y 126 to 436, cell 62.
4. Fill the seven piece edges into both halves, 14 cells: `{s,a}`, `{s,b}`, `{a,b}`, `{a,c}`, `{b,c}`, `{c,t}`, `{a,t}`.
5. Draw the diagonal from the grid's top right corner to its bottom left corner, 1.6, dash 6 4, and place the two half labels outside the cells they name.
6. Build view C: headers at y 508, list row at y 542, matrix row at y 578, space values at x 380, query values at x 700, graph label at x 380 on a leader.
7. Route every paint through a CSS custom property set in `:root` and in `.dark`; no raw hex in the drawing.
8. Run the audit and the DOM checks below, then hand the sheet to a non-author read.

- interaction: none, static sheet, no hover, no focus target.
- animation: none, the sheet has one state.
- ids and classes: `#fig-09-02`, `.panel`, `.key`, `.row`, `.cell-filled`, `.cell-empty`, `.diag`, `.pair`, `.asym`, `.note`.
- verification hooks: `data-graph="sample-8"`, `data-count-list="16"`, `data-count-matrix="64"`, `data-cells="25"`, `data-filled="12"`, so a DOM assertion reads the numbers the strip prints.
- commands: `python3 /home/carloshnp/.hermes/skills/svg/illustration-spec/scripts/check_spec.py /home/carloshnp/study-site/docs/figures/dsa-09-graphs/FIG-09-02-representations.md`, then `grep -nP "[\x{2013}\x{2014}]"` on the built SVG, then the element count per view against the floors.

## 9b. Mechanism and state model

- STATE: none: every coordinate is placed, nothing is derived
- DATUM: not applicable, no derived coordinate
- INV-01 The grid holds 25 cells at every build, five per row and five per column, and its headers name the same five vertices as view A.
- INV-02 The filled cells number twice the seven piece edges, 14, and every filled cell has a filled mirror across the diagonal.
- INV-03 Both numbers in a printed pair come from the graph named beside that pair, so the strip prints 16 against 64 on the sample graph and never a count taken from the piece.
- FUTURE STATE: a later revision may print the piece's own pair, 12 entries against 25 cells, inside views A and B; the strip keeps the whole graph pair, and the two scopes stay labelled apart.

